\details{
A traversal walks a route in flow order. A question is shown if the route
always shows it, or if its display logic holds given the answers drawn so
far. A trigger question gets a state only when it is shown: hidden means
unanswered; shown with a forced response (\code{ForceResponse = "ON"}, when
\code{respect_force_response = TRUE}) means answered; otherwise answered or
unanswered. Each state of a trigger, and each iteration count of a Loop &
Merge block (\code{loop_weights}, equal by default), is a branch. Inside a loop,
triggers in the same block are drawn afresh in each iteration; other
triggers keep one state.

\strong{Weights.} Every route has equal total weight. With \code{method = "exact"}
every branch combination is enumerated and weighted by the product of its
branch weights, so the weights reproduce the structural convention of
\code{\link[=path_burden_profile]{path_burden_profile()}}. With \code{method = "sample"}, \code{n_draws} traversals are
drawn per route (stratified by route), each with weight
\code{1 / (n_routes * n_draws)}. \code{"auto"} enumerates when the number of
traversals is at most \code{max_traversals}, and samples otherwise. The method
used per route is in \code{attr(x, "method")}. These are model weights, not
observed frequencies.

\strong{Derived fields.} \code{field_rules} derives stored fields from the answers
drawn in a traversal, for values that survey code (for example page
JavaScript) computes and the survey file does not record. Each element,
named by its target (\code{"@Field"} for a stored field, \code{"@n:<BL_id>"} for a
loop's iteration count), is a function of the traversal state, or
\code{list(fun = , inputs = )} naming questions the rule reads that are not
display-logic triggers (these are then drawn when shown: every choice,
plus unanswered unless forced). In the state, a loop question's answer in
iteration \code{j} is \code{"<QID>#<j>"} and a loop count is \code{"@n:<BL_id>"}. A
derived field is never enumerated; its value is set before any display
logic that reads it. A rule returning \code{NA} rejects the traversal: exact
weights are renormalised within the route, rejected draws are redrawn, and
the rejected share per route is in \code{attr(x, "rejected")}. A loop-count
rule makes routes that contain its loop sampled (\code{loop_weights} for that loop
are then ignored, with a warning). A display condition that reads a
question later in the flow is listed in \code{attr(x, "unresolved")} as a
forward reference; that question is read as unanswered.

\strong{Speed.} A rule is called once per distinct combination of its inputs,
and results are reused across chunks and routes. Rules with many inputs
(for example an answer in each of ten loop iterations) are faster when
vectorised: \code{list(fun = , inputs = , vectorised = TRUE)} calls
\code{fun} once per chunk with whole columns (\code{""} for unanswered,
choice ids otherwise; loop counts as integers, \code{NULL} when the route
has no such loop) and expects one value per traversal.

Differences from \code{\link[=path_burden_profile]{path_burden_profile()}}: no primary-gate approximation;
a conditionally shown forced trigger never has an unanswered state; and
\code{field_rules} apply. Without these, exact traversal quantiles equal the
profile's.
